In the exact setting/notation of JAC-AFFINE-DESCENT, the named sub-result states:

For a proper native intermediate, ANY geometric A1-ruling over an AFFINE smooth curve is unique up to a base automorphism, descends to K0, and admits a nonzero locally nilpotent derivation defined over K0.

The descent is constructive at the level of its coordinate:

choose f over a finite Galois extension L/K0;
    f0=f-f(P);
    choose c in L with h=sum_sigma sigma(c*f0) nonzero;
    then h belongs to A and gives the descended A1-ruling. (2.1)

There is no assumption that a chosen geometric ruling was already Galois invariant. We prove that invariance from (1.1).

### 2.1 Affine bases and a volume-defined additive action

First work geometrically and let phi:V_geometric->C be a surjective A1-ruling with C affine. The dominant rational map from U to C makes the projective completion of C rational. Constant geometric units exclude two or more boundary points; affineness supplies at least one. Thus C=A1. Write phi=f.

In the containing manuscript setting and definitions, the exact declared statement is:


The map $F_G$ satisfies
\[
 \det DF_G=-2,
 \qquad
 \gdeg(F_G)=N.
\]
Its generic inverse equation is $E_{\Pi,B,C}(S)=0$.  Fix
$(\pi,b,c)\in K^*\times K^2$, put $E=E_{\pi,b,c}$, and let
$\overline{E'}$ be the class of $E'$ in $K[S]/(E)$.  Then
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 F_G^{-1}(\pi,b,c)
 \simeq
 \operatorname{Spec}\left((K[S]/(E))[1/\overline{E'}]\right).}

\end{equation}
Equivalently, for every commutative $K$-algebra $A$ there is a natural
bijection
\begin{equation}
 \operatorname{Hom}_{K\text{-alg}}
 \left((K[S]/(E))[1/\overline{E'}],A\right)
 \simeq \mathcal F_{\pi,b,c}(A).

\end{equation}

In the containing manuscript setting and definitions, the exact declared statement is:


Let \(A\) be a finite \'etale algebra of rank at least three over a number
field.  Any realization supplied by
Corollary~\ref{cor:finite-etale-realization} has fiber \(\Spec A\) itself.
It therefore preserves:
\begin{itemize}
\item connectedness and field decomposition;
\item real signature;
\item splitting field and Galois action on geometric points;
\item the local algebras \(A\otimes_KK_v\) at every place;
\item factorization types for integral models after also excluding the
primes needed to spread out the displayed algebra isomorphism;
\item the existence or nonexistence of rational and local points.
\end{itemize}
For the integral assertion, transport the chosen order through the algebra
isomorphism. Comparing it with an independently chosen order requires
inverting the finite set of primes at which the orders or the isomorphism
do not extend. Only after this localization are the reduction algebras
identified.
Thus an arithmetic example built as a finite \'etale algebra of rank at least
three can be carried, without alteration, into a full Keller fiber.
Proposition~\ref{prop:base-change} gives the corresponding compatibility with
local fields and other scalar extensions.

In the containing manuscript setting and definitions, the exact declared statement is:


Let \(N_{\Phi,\ell}(B)\) count the Hasse-failing targets on \(\ell\) of
height at most \(B\). Then
\begin{equation}
 N_{\Phi,\ell}(B)\sim C_\Phi\frac{B^2}{\log B},\qquad
 C_\Phi=\frac{11}{288\sqrt3\,\pi^2}
 \prod_{p\equiv2\ (3)}\frac{1+p^{-3}}{1-p^{-3}}>0.

\end{equation}
The Euler product converges absolutely.

In the containing manuscript setting and definitions, the exact declared statement is:


Writing \(M_\Phi(B)=\#\{a\in\mathcal A:H(y_a)\le B\}\), we have
\begin{equation}
 M_\Phi(B)\sim\frac{G_3(1)}{96\sqrt\pi}
 \frac{B}{\sqrt{\log B}},

\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
 G_3(1)=\left[\frac23L(1,\chi_{-3})
 \prod_{p\equiv2\ (3)}(1-p^{-2})\right]^{1/2}.

\end{equation}

